WEBVTT

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:36.300
Have you felt any movement?

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:38.000
No, not yet.

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:39.030
Is that okay?

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:40.190
I mean, does that mean...

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:42.550
All it means is it's too soon, but you will.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.880
You're not picking up any spare change or keys, are you?

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.420
Because, and I mean this in a completely loving way,

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.740
but my wife has been eating everything that's not locked now.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:53.310
Heart function is good.

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.520
Kidney development right on schedule.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:58.910
What can I say?

00:00:59.630 --> 00:00:59.970
He looks...

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.750
Hey, you mean that in a generic, non-sex-specific way, right?

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:10.190
Kind of like how you refer to a ship as she.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:12.950
No, kind of the way you refer to a baby with a penis.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.040
Oh, I thought you said you wanted to know.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:22.740
God, did I misread the notation on your file?

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:24.380
My handwriting is terrible.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:24.720
No!

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:29.300
No, we were just expecting one of those drumroll moments.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:29.660
Right.

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:33.540
Well, and, uh, da-da-dun-da-dun.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:34.730
It's a boy.

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:39.800
I feel like we're having a ball.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:43.510
See it?

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:44.690
Right there.

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:45.010
See?

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.900
That's so tiny.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:50.010
So teeny, teeny, tiny.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:51.360
Barely anything.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:52.720
Well, hey, it'll get bigger.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:54.100
Yeah, I hope so.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.000
Because kids can be really cool.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.420
So, what you gonna name him?

00:01:59.440 --> 00:01:59.880
Have you decided?

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.520
Well, it's a pretty short list.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:10.500
You know, Jared Boffman's parents, they let him name his baby brother Fritzie.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:11.690
Fritzie?

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.890
Well, Fritz. It's his dad's name.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.810
Thought it'd be kinda cool to name the kid after, you know, a relative.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.240
Well, that's sort of along the same lines that we're thinking of for this baby.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:25.100
But, you know, if you wanted to pick the name, Owen...

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:26.780
Really? You'd let me do that?

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:28.120
Really? You'd let him do that?

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:29.670
Yeah, I mean, I think...

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.920
It would mean a lot to him to be able to say that his uncle Owen gave him his name,

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:37.840
and that the name, it had a very, very special meaning for both of you.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.760
Kind of like you naming Diane after Mom.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:43.100
Exactly.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:46.410
What's going on?

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:47.250
Is everything okay?

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:48.430
Of course.

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:50.770
What, I can't treat my brother to a nice lunch?

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:51.620
Of course you can.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:52.480
You absolutely can.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:54.520
It's just in 17 years, you haven't.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:59.960
You had this sort of, I gotta tell you something, tone in your...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:15.180
voice on the phone. Yeah, I did. I do. Bailey, I'm going to Juilliard in the fall. What? I thought

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:20.890
you accepted at Stanford already. I did, but just because it was, well, safe and completely doable,

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:25.930
but I mean, Juilliard, they're offering me a pretty okay financial aid package. They've got

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:29.480
a great work-study program, and I want this.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:32.920
Really, it just, it feels right.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:39.320
Oh well, you could say something.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:44.200
You might want to budget some train tickets

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:45.640
into the financial equation.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.360
I'm sorry, you want me to take trains back and forth

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:49.080
to San Francisco?

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:53.210
No, not San Francisco, not home, Philadelphia.

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:54.460
Philadelphia?

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:56.430
I don't know anyone in Philadelphia.

00:03:58.520 --> 00:03:59.020
You know me.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:02.000
So, that's Charlie.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:04.240
Claude's free. I just gotta check with Bailey.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:10.040
Why don't we just tell everybody a little bit more casually, you know, bump into Charlie, break the good news, say,

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.800
don't tell Bailey because I want to tell him myself, and so on?

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:15.330
No. I want to make it a big deal.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.760
It is a big deal. We're moving across the country, for God's sake.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:20.820
So I'm starting a new job. You're starting at Georgetown.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:22.720
I want to make an event out of this, you know?

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:25.170
What?

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:28.810
You know you're being a little bit manic about this.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:38.330
Okay, look, I just, um, I don't want to have a series of heart-to-hearts.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:41.980
I don't want to talk about the pros and cons of leaving everyone behind.

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:44.420
I'm not going to treat this like a mixed blessing.

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:47.510
I want to stand up at Salinger's with a smile on my face and say,

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:50.450
hey, guess what I'm leaving to do, and guess who's coming with me,

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:54.230
and you know what, I'm really excited, and you all don't have a choice but to be excited for me.

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:55.390
You know?

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:57.160
Gotcha.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:04:59.880
So, what's the big mystery?

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.180
Okay, okay, I have a little announcement to make.

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:04.300
Come on, let's hear it.

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:05.480
No, not too fast.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:07.440
You guys order your food and relax.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:08.560
I want to build the suspense.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:10.480
So what's everybody having, huh?

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:15.050
Hey, Jewel, before you get started, I have something to tell you, too.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:17.910
I kind of have a little news of my own, and everybody else already knows.

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:18.960
What?

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:23.200
In a couple months, I'm going to be leaving to go to school in Philadelphia.

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:29.540
I'm going to get an undergraduate degree in business at the University of Pennsylvania.

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:30.860
Wait, you're leaving?

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:31.720
Yeah.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:33.960
And everyone else knew this?

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:37.560
I had to work it out with Charlie and Owen first, and I just told Claudia this morning.

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:38.790
Nigel, what's the problem?

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:41.200
The problem is you can't be doing that.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:42.760
Bailey, that's what I'm doing.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:44.190
That's my news.

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:44.670
I'm leaving.

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:46.590
What?

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:49.970
I got offered a job in Washington.

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:52.830
I'm going to be an intern for the National Organization for Women.

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:54.770
Justin is coming with me.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:56.050
You're already transferred to Georgetown.

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:57.970
I thought you guys were getting engaged.

00:05:58.400 --> 00:05:58.880
You engaged?

00:05:58.980 --> 00:05:59.900
What are you, clueless, Justin?

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.760
just got separated a month ago i know that so we're all leaving what what do you mean we're all

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:12.720
leaving i'm going to juilliard what about stanford i changed my mind wait wait a second wait just a

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:17.320
second no no this is getting a little out of hand here bailey and i talked about him leaving so

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:22.220
so what does that matter he talked to you first so he gets to go i mean how is that fair mine

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:27.860
is set already well some is mine bailey mine too hey what about me who's gonna stay with me

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:29.020
Charlie if you all go

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:56.760
Like, we've got a problem, you know.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:06:59.920
Kidding.

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:08.720
I mean, whenever one of us goes away, the rest of us, we just kind of, I don't know, close the circle, wait for the person to come back.

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:13.530
I mean, we've dealt with that plenty of times, but this, this is different.

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:16.330
What do you want me to say?

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:20.830
Just think about it, Bailey.

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:27.020
Look, you got a great internship. I understand that, okay?

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:29.980
But you know that you could get another one.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:35.190
just like it next year. You know that. You have a great resume. You can get anything you want,

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:49.380
Julie. Anything. Well, I'm not like that. I need help. I have to get lucky. Well, this guy

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:54.200
that Charlie knows, he's pulling strings for me big time. I won't get a chance

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:58.690
at a business degree from an Ivy League school ever again.

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:02.670
ever again, if I give this up.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:09.070
I told them yes in Washington.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:12.050
Really, I told my school.

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:18.340
I'm always the one that backs down.

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:25.380
Julie, I'm always the one that gets stuck here giving in this time.

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:39.390
Worried about all the wrong things, didn't I?

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:40.920
What do you mean?

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:44.330
Spent all this time trying to work things out with Bailey.

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:47.120
Trying to make peace with the idea of him leaving.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:49.920
Negotiating all the little details.

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:51.280
Owen and the house.

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:55.900
What it would be like for me and Julia and Claudia to be without him.

00:08:58.300 --> 00:08:59.580
What a complete waste of time.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:07.620
It's really incredible how things work out, isn't it?

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:13.720
I mean, way back in the beginning, right after mom and dad died,

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:20.330
before that too, I mean, I was the one who didn't give a damn about any of this.

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:25.850
Family, God, I didn't want any of it.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:29.820
Now I'm the only one who's left.

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:36.760
I don't know what I'm going to do.

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:01.460
What did he say?

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:32.900
You using the washer still? I got a load here.

00:10:34.450 --> 00:10:37.240
Yeah, that's fine. You stayed over, huh?

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:39.510
Yep.

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:46.660
Look, I'm sure you and Julia talked about what she and I did.

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:49.620
You don't have to say anything. You don't have to explain it.

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:52.510
You got what you wanted, right?

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:53.820
What?

00:10:57.140 --> 00:10:58.100
She didn't tell you yet.

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:01.080
She's not going.

00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:05.090
She put a call into Washington this morning, turning down her job.

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:08.300
So, you go, she stays, congratulations.

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:19.120
Justin, when I applied, I had no idea...

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:21.060
All right, all right, look, I know, Bailey, I know.

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:24.420
This is the biggest opportunity you've ever had, right?

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:26.740
She explained it all very clearly.

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:29.960
Well, the problem is, is that this is not just...

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.320
just about who makes the best career move.

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:33.490
Meaning what?

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:36.360
Meaning it is also about her and me,

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:39.190
about being in love and getting out of here.

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:41.660
Why do you have to get out of here?

00:11:41.960 --> 00:11:42.500
Are you kidding?

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:46.310
Because of all of you, because when she's here,

00:11:46.490 --> 00:11:48.050
she can't get away from her family.

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:51.230
I mean, for God's sakes, look at the decision

00:11:51.230 --> 00:11:54.690
that she just made, throwing away all of her plans

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:55.630
just for you.

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:09.770
You know, a good school is not the only chance in life that doesn't come around twice.

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:21.370
Hey, Troy, I decided on a name for the baby.

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:25.270
You have?

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:28.870
Yeah, I thought a lot about what you said.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:30.600
You did.

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:34.150
How would it be good to name him after someone who matters to me?

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:37.350
Someone who I kind of owe a lot to.

00:12:38.380 --> 00:12:38.700
Okay.

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:42.000
I think I know where this is going, but lay it on me.

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:43.560
Let's do it.

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:49.960
I'm sorry, Stewie?

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:52.060
After Stewie the Tilo.

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:53.480
He's on my soccer team.

00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:56.180
Stewie, you want to name the baby Stewie?

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:56.520
Sure.

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:58.780
Stewie the Tilo is, like, amazing.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.040
He scored five goals last Saturday.

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:05.080
He was really good at flicking boogers across the room.

00:13:05.620 --> 00:13:07.280
Wouldn't that be cool if the baby could do that?

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:10.120
But you're indebted to him how, exactly?

00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:15.300
Owen, you said you wanted to name the baby after someone you owe a lot to.

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:17.660
But you said I could pick.

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:24.850
I can't let you do that.

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:27.520
It's not your decision, Bailey. It's mine.

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:29.980
Yeah, but you want to go to Juilliard.

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:32.660
Claudie, I just sat across from you at lunch yesterday.

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:33.920
Yeah, so...

00:13:34.764 --> 00:13:36.124
Maybe that's not where I'm meant to be.

00:13:37.932 --> 00:13:41.412
Maybe the greatest teacher I'll ever have is sitting in a classroom at Stanford right now.

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:46.060
Maybe my closest lifelong friend has got her acceptance there, too. You never know.

00:13:47.948 --> 00:13:49.088
Is that really what you think?

00:13:50.700 --> 00:13:51.980
That it doesn't matter where you go.

00:13:52.144 --> 00:13:53.684
Your first choice, your second choice.

00:13:59.500 --> 00:14:00.000
Here's a thing.

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:07.700
For the last six years, it's been pretty much a one-way street, with you taking care of me.

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:14.930
No, that's not true. It went both-

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:18.600
Both ways. Even, Claude.

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:20.480
Not even close.

00:14:20.780 --> 00:14:25.640
You and Charlie and Julia stayed here and kept us together.

00:14:25.880 --> 00:14:27.920
For me and for Owen.

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:31.580
year 16.

00:14:34.988 --> 00:14:35.968
Younger than I am now.

00:14:38.892 --> 00:14:40.892
all that for me.

00:14:44.396 --> 00:14:45.396
It's my turn.

00:14:55.884 --> 00:14:56.064
Hey.

00:14:57.036 --> 00:14:59.536
Paying $38.25 for a flat Brazilian cherry.

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:03.450
Yeah, well, Stamson raised his prices.

00:15:03.730 --> 00:15:06.490
Well, he still owes us eight flats from March.

00:15:07.400 --> 00:15:08.840
Make sure you hold them to the old price.

00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:10.720
What is this?

00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:13.940
You take the kid out of the business, but you can't take the business out of the kid?

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:17.500
Something like that, yeah.

00:15:21.140 --> 00:15:23.740
Just don't be surprised when I come crawling back here.

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:29.980
I had a nice couple of days there thinking I could...

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:34.560
I could actually take off, head back east.

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:36.340
What are you talking about?

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:41.760
If I go, then everything unravels for Julie and Claudia.

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:44.270
They won't go if I go.

00:15:45.350 --> 00:15:47.060
Wait, they said that?

00:15:47.490 --> 00:15:49.290
Claudia told me she's saying yes to Stanford,

00:15:49.450 --> 00:15:53.060
and Justin said that Julia's backing up.

00:15:57.580 --> 00:15:57.760
Nah.

00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:03.210
Why are you staying?

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:09.090
I tried saying, no, forget it.

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:11.180
This is my turn to leave.

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:19.900
But to be honest here, if the guilt didn't kill me,

00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:23.400
it'd just get me flunked out and sent back home anyway.

00:16:24.070 --> 00:16:27.320
And by that time, Julie will have lost this internship

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:29.420
and they would have given Claude's spot at Juilliard away.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:31.420
So I'm just going to bow out now.

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:35.660
And I thought you should know.

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:38.280
Bailey, I mean, maybe there's a way.

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:39.100
You know what?

00:16:41.350 --> 00:16:45.710
It's not really such a horrible thing to put them first.

00:16:45.910 --> 00:16:49.780
I mean, I love them.

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:55.920
I'll get my chance some other time.

00:16:58.030 --> 00:16:58.590
Bailey, listen.

00:16:59.080 --> 00:16:59.300
Charlie.

00:17:02.270 --> 00:17:04.790
You don't need to say anything. It's okay.

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:16.280
I mean, no, obviously it's not okay, but it's how it is.

00:17:19.320 --> 00:17:24.270
We've had the chance to say that about a lot of things for a long time now, haven't we?

00:17:26.750 --> 00:17:28.390
It's not okay. It's just how it is.

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:38.200
Anyway, I'll see you.

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:52.720
Charlie?

00:17:56.850 --> 00:17:57.130
Bailey?

00:17:58.400 --> 00:17:59.140
Charlie, what's going on?

00:17:59.300 --> 00:17:59.980
I need to...

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:29.760
talk to you. Is something wrong? Is he here? What happened? Something happened? Charlie, is Kristen okay? She's fine. It's, uh, it's what? Look, uh, I do this thing after all these years. It's kind of a reflex where I ask myself what they would do. What answer would they have if three of their kids came to them with a

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:35.880
A good reason to leave, a great opportunity that was going to take them far away, what would they say?

00:18:42.100 --> 00:18:47.210
They'd say go. You should go, the three of you.

00:18:48.420 --> 00:18:51.510
No, they wouldn't. They would want us to stay together.

00:18:53.290 --> 00:18:55.310
We talked about this already. We can't all leave.

